  "worked_solution": "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {t}^{3}+\\beta {t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=3\\alpha {t}^{2}+2\\beta t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=6\\alpha t+2\\beta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}(6\\alpha t+2\\beta )+2t(3\\alpha {t}^{2}+2\\beta t) & =12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2} \\\\ (6\\alpha +6\\alpha ){t}^{3}+(2\\beta +4\\beta ){t}^{2} & =12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2} \\\\ 12\\alpha {t}^{3}+6\\beta {t}^{2} & =12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{t}^{3}) \\\\ 6\\beta =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\,\\beta =-4.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}.\n$$",
